  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late \nTuesday evening reveal weak ridging is in place from the \nsouthern plains through the central plains with a significant, \ncut-off trough centered just offshore off the west coast. At the\nsurface, broad high pressure reaches from its center over \nsouthern Quebec, Canada, southwestward through the central \nplains, resulting in modest southerly winds across southwest KS.\nFor the remainder of the overnight period, 10-15 mph winds and \npartly to mostly cloudy skies will support Wednesday morning \nlows in the low to mid 50s. Daytime Wednesday, short range \nguidance indicates the strong cut-off low out west will make \nlittle progress eastward as the downstream ridge amplifies \nconsiderably, extending through the northern plains and into \nsouth-central Canada. As a result, surface high pressure over \nour area will be reinforced, yielding light and variable winds \nand a slight uptick in afternoon temperatures with highs in the \nmid to upper 70s. Wednesday night, light winds and mostly clear \nskies will permit lows dropping into the upper 40s to low 50s.\n\nVery little change to the synoptic pattern is forecast on \nThursday as the high amplitude trough out west and the ridge \noverhead inch eastward. Nevertheless, lee cyclogenesis will \nbegin in east-central MT and adjacent areas, which will engender\nmore robust southerly winds across southwest KS in the 15-25 \nmph range gusting to 30-35 mph. Additionally, 850-mb \ntemperatures are progged to increase by 2- 4 degrees C, which \nwill translate to afternoon highs reaching the upper 70s/low \n80s.\n\nFriday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nagree the strong upper wave will stall out over the central \nRockies before a shortwave trough diving southeast from south-\ncentral Canada pushes it onto the High Plains over the weekend \nand into early next week. This synoptic evolution will support a\nsteady, moderate cooling trend as afternoon temperatures fall \nfrom the upper 70s on Friday to the upper 50s/low 60s Tuesday \nand beyond. Furthermore, the slow progression of the upper wave \nis ideal for precipitation across southwest KS, and this is \nindeed reflected by the NBM, which paints plenty of chance \n(25-54%) to likely (55-74%) pops across much of our area \nSaturday and Sunday, as well as the ECMWF ENS and GEFS mean \ntotal precipitation fields which are in the 1-2\" and 0.75-1.5\" \nrange, respectively, through next Monday evening.",
